title: Running inside a container
tags: Security
kind: guide
audience: dev
summary: When the container forbids WebKit's sandbox, --allow-unsandboxed-webview lets the GUI start, for development only.

WebKit's sandbox mounts `/proc`, which some container policies refuse; the GUI
then cannot confine its web process and refuses to start
([[Sandboxing the media decoders]]). For development inside such a container:

```
metafolder-gui --allow-unsandboxed-webview
```

It prints a loud warning at every start. It is a per-run flag with no
`config.toml` equivalent, so it cannot rot into a permanently disabled sandbox.
Images and video are then decoded with your full privileges: a crafted file can
take over the session. The media helpers stay sandboxed regardless — they avoid
`/proc`, and work there.

Another container trap: `bwrap --disable-userns` writes
`/proc/sys/user/max_user_namespaces`, which is read-only in some containers. That
is why namespace creation is refused by the syscall filter instead.
